🌩️ AWS Storage: EBS vs EFS vs S3 – Simplified for Azure Pros! 🌩️
As started my cloud journey with Azure, I got familiar with two main types of storage for VMs:
1️⃣ Azure Managed Disks (SSD/HDD – block storage for VMs)
2️⃣ Azure Files (file share, part of Azure’s storage account)
So when I started diving into AWS, I used to map my Azure knowledge to AWS services.

🔹 EBS (Elastic Block Store)
- Azure equivalent: Managed Disks 
- A virtual hard drive(SSD/HDD or Premium drives) which can mounted on a single EC2 instance. 
- It can be used to install OS, Database, Applications & their dependencies.

🔹 EFS (Elastic File System)
Azure equivalent: Azure Files(Part of Azure’s storage account)
- A NFS(Network File System) share which can be shared across multiple ec2 instances using NFS or SMB protocol.   
- Ideal for Content management systems, data analytics or applications requiring shared file access.

🔹 S3 (Simple Storage Service)
Azure equivalent: Blob Storage (Part of Azure’s storage account)
- Object storage designed for storing and retrieving large amounts of unstructured data from anywhere on the web (like Image, video, or audio files for websites and apps). 
- Usually used for Backups, static website hosting, data archiving, and content distribution. 


Relating cloud concepts across platforms makes learning new tools so much easier! If you’re transitioning from Azure to AWS—or vice versa— I hope this helps clarify where to store what.
